1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,956
You want to learn Linux, but you do not want to break your own computer.

2
00:00:04,506 --> 00:00:07,493
The answer is a disposable Linux virtual machine.

3
00:00:08,043 --> 00:00:10,539
Break it, delete it, and start fresh in minutes.

4
00:00:11,089 --> 00:00:14,481
Today: why, your options, the install, and how to log in.

5
00:00:16,531 --> 00:00:18,088
First, why disposable?

6
00:00:18,638 --> 00:00:23,332
A virtual machine is separate from your real computer, so a mistake stays inside it.

7
00:00:23,882 --> 00:00:27,082
It is the same for everyone, so your steps match the lesson.

8
00:00:27,632 --> 00:00:29,936
And if it breaks, you delete it, and rebuild.

9
00:00:30,486 --> 00:00:32,491
Treat it like a sandbox, not a home.

10
00:00:34,541 --> 00:00:36,418
Where does the virtual machine come from?

11
00:00:36,968 --> 00:00:41,491
On your own computer, VirtualBox gives you a window, and a normal installer.

12
00:00:42,041 --> 00:00:45,220
Multipass starts an Ubuntu machine with one command.

13
00:00:45,770 --> 00:00:49,866
On Windows, WSL 2 gives you a light Linux environment.

14
00:00:50,416 --> 00:00:55,920
Or rent one in the cloud, from a provider such as AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud.

15
00:00:56,470 --> 00:00:59,883
Cloud machines can cost money, so delete them when you are done.

16
00:01:00,433 --> 00:01:02,886
Any of them works. Pick one, and stay with it.

17
00:01:04,936 --> 00:01:06,557
Next: how big should it be?

18
00:01:07,107 --> 00:01:07,892
For practice,

19
00:01:07,892 --> 00:01:08,797
two processors,

20
00:01:08,797 --> 00:01:10,245
two gigabytes of memory,

21
00:01:10,245 --> 00:01:13,443
and twenty gigabytes of disk are a comfortable start.

22
00:01:13,993 --> 00:01:16,319
Never give it more than your computer can spare.

23
00:01:16,869 --> 00:01:23,290
Choose a long term support release of Ubuntu Server, because it is widely documented.

24
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:28,448
Server means no desktop, just the command line, which is exactly what we want.

25
00:01:30,498 --> 00:01:32,418
Here is the install, in two ways.

26
00:01:32,968 --> 00:01:36,419
With VirtualBox: download the Ubuntu Server ISO,

27
00:01:36,419 --> 00:01:38,864
create a machine with those sizes,

28
00:01:38,864 --> 00:01:40,733
and start it from the ISO.

29
00:01:41,283 --> 00:01:43,155
The installer asks for a disk,

30
00:01:43,155 --> 00:01:43,904
a user name,

31
00:01:43,904 --> 00:01:44,840
and a password.

32
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:46,337
Choose a throwaway user,

33
00:01:46,337 --> 00:01:47,897
and a throwaway password.

34
00:01:48,447 --> 00:01:53,844
Tick the option to install the OpenSSH server, so you can log in remotely later.

35
00:01:54,394 --> 00:01:56,890
With Multipass, one command does all of it.

36
00:01:57,440 --> 00:01:58,997
Launch creates the machine,

37
00:01:58,997 --> 00:02:00,900
shell opens a terminal inside it,

38
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:02,688
and list shows what is running.

39
00:02:04,738 --> 00:02:06,167
Now: how do you get in?

40
00:02:06,717 --> 00:02:11,005
The console is the machine's own screen. It always works, but it is awkward.

41
00:02:11,555 --> 00:02:15,203
SSH lets you log in from your own terminal, over the network.

42
00:02:15,753 --> 00:02:20,852
SSH needs three things: an address, a user name, and a way to prove who you are.

43
00:02:21,402 --> 00:02:25,006
The best proof is a key pair: a private key that stays secret,

44
00:02:25,006 --> 00:02:27,738
and a public key that you place on the machine.

45
00:02:28,288 --> 00:02:29,824
Never share the private key.

46
00:02:31,874 --> 00:02:36,610
Now let us do the SSH part for real, in a disposable Linux sandbox.

47
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:39,429
These outputs are real captures.

48
00:02:39,429 --> 00:02:39,784
Here,

49
00:02:39,784 --> 00:02:42,549
the sandbox plays your virtual machine,

50
00:02:42,549 --> 00:02:44,819
and SSH connects back to itself.

51
00:02:45,369 --> 00:02:49,123
First, we create a key pair. The command stays quiet when it works.

52
00:02:49,673 --> 00:02:54,665
The fingerprint identifies the key, and it is safe to share. The private file is not.

53
00:02:55,215 --> 00:02:56,763
Now we log in with the key,

54
00:02:56,763 --> 00:02:58,940
and run three commands: the host name,

55
00:02:58,940 --> 00:02:59,743
the user name,

56
00:02:59,743 --> 00:03:01,061
and the kernel version.

57
00:03:01,611 --> 00:03:06,731
The answers come from the machine we logged in to: its name, its user, and its kernel.

58
00:03:07,281 --> 00:03:10,950
If we try the wrong port, nothing answers: connection refused.

59
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:15,639
In your own machine, you use its address, and the port is usually twenty-two.

60
00:03:17,689 --> 00:03:19,246
Three messages you will meet.

61
00:03:19,796 --> 00:03:22,340
Connection refused: the machine answered,

62
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:24,697
but nothing is listening on that port.

63
00:03:24,697 --> 00:03:26,496
Is SSH installed and running,

64
00:03:26,496 --> 00:03:27,860
and is the port right?

65
00:03:28,410 --> 00:03:30,772
Permission denied: the server is there,

66
00:03:30,772 --> 00:03:33,376
but it rejected your key or your user name.

67
00:03:33,376 --> 00:03:34,042
Check both.

68
00:03:34,592 --> 00:03:36,798
Connection timed out: no answer at all.

69
00:03:36,798 --> 00:03:38,720
Check that the machine is running,

70
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:40,416
and that the address is right.

71
00:03:40,966 --> 00:03:43,612
Read the message, then check one thing at a time.

72
00:03:45,662 --> 00:03:47,283
Now the disposable part.

73
00:03:47,833 --> 00:03:49,454
Before a risky experiment,

74
00:03:49,454 --> 00:03:50,452
take a snapshot.

75
00:03:50,452 --> 00:03:52,945
VirtualBox can save the machine's state,

76
00:03:52,945 --> 00:03:54,255
and restore it later.

77
00:03:54,805 --> 00:03:58,303
With Multipass, delete the machine, purge it, and launch it again.

78
00:03:58,853 --> 00:04:03,120
Keep anything valuable outside the virtual machine, such as scripts in Git.

79
00:04:03,670 --> 00:04:07,083
If you can rebuild it in minutes, you can experiment without fear.

80
00:04:09,133 --> 00:04:10,989
Watch out for four common myths.

81
00:04:11,539 --> 00:04:15,071
Myth one: a virtual machine puts my computer at risk.

82
00:04:15,071 --> 00:04:15,604
In fact,

83
00:04:15,604 --> 00:04:18,003
it is the safer place to experiment.

84
00:04:18,553 --> 00:04:21,127
Myth two: I need a powerful computer.

85
00:04:21,127 --> 00:04:21,683
In fact,

86
00:04:21,683 --> 00:04:24,953
a small Ubuntu Server runs on modest resources.

87
00:04:25,503 --> 00:04:30,644
Myth three: a password is just as good as a key. In fact, keys are much harder to guess.

88
00:04:31,194 --> 00:04:34,536
Myth four: a disposable machine can hold my real work.

89
00:04:34,536 --> 00:04:35,031
In fact,

90
00:04:35,031 --> 00:04:36,331
if you might need it,

91
00:04:36,331 --> 00:04:37,445
keep it elsewhere.

92
00:04:37,995 --> 00:04:39,830
Disposable means replaceable.

93
00:04:41,880 --> 00:04:43,608
Here is how you check your own work.

94
00:04:44,158 --> 00:04:47,337
Imagine a teammate asks you to set up a practice machine.

95
00:04:47,887 --> 00:04:49,785
Explain why it is disposable.

96
00:04:50,335 --> 00:04:52,191
Choose a platform, and say why.

97
00:04:52,741 --> 00:04:54,576
Create it with sensible sizes.

98
00:04:55,126 --> 00:04:57,195
Log in over SSH, with a key.

99
00:04:57,745 --> 00:04:59,473
Verify it with three commands.

100
00:05:00,023 --> 00:05:01,559
Delete it, and rebuild it.

101
00:05:02,109 --> 00:05:03,731
To pass, you need all six.

102
00:05:05,781 --> 00:05:06,656
Your turn.

103
00:05:07,206 --> 00:05:10,166
This lab is unverified until you run it yourself,

104
00:05:10,166 --> 00:05:13,307
because I cannot install a machine on your computer.

105
00:05:13,857 --> 00:05:16,375
One: create a machine with two processors,

106
00:05:16,375 --> 00:05:17,814
two gigabytes of memory,

107
00:05:17,814 --> 00:05:19,553
and twenty gigabytes of disk.

108
00:05:20,103 --> 00:05:22,492
Two: log in over SSH, with a key.

109
00:05:23,042 --> 00:05:24,595
Three: run the host name,

110
00:05:24,595 --> 00:05:25,216
user name,

111
00:05:25,216 --> 00:05:26,955
and kernel version commands,

112
00:05:26,955 --> 00:05:28,632
and write down the answers.

113
00:05:29,182 --> 00:05:33,811
Four: break something inside it on purpose, then delete the machine, and rebuild it.

114
00:05:34,361 --> 00:05:35,961
Mark yourself on the rubric.

115
00:05:36,511 --> 00:05:38,239
Pause the video, and do it now.

116
00:05:51,789 --> 00:05:52,664
Quick check.

117
00:05:53,214 --> 00:05:55,048
Why use a disposable machine?

118
00:05:55,598 --> 00:05:57,134
Name two ways to get one.

119
00:05:57,684 --> 00:05:58,922
What does a key pair do?

120
00:05:59,472 --> 00:06:01,349
What does connection refused mean?

121
00:06:01,899 --> 00:06:06,400
Objective covered: MC02-O05.

122
00:06:06,950 --> 00:06:09,020
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

123
